  "live_records": {
    "q-143301": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143301",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(5,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 14,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(70\\le Y\\le 78),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ | $\\,0.8599\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ | $\\,0.8810\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{14} & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(14\\cdot 5,\\,14\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(70,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(70\\le Y\\le 78).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(70\\le Y\\le 78) & =P(\\frac{70-70}{\\sqrt{56}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{78-70}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(0\\le Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.07)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.07)=0.8577.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(70\\le Y\\le 78)=Φ(1.07)-0.5=0.8577-0.5=0.3577.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
