    "q-152011": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152011",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 18,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(16\\le Y\\le 20),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{18} & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(18\\cdot 1,\\,18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =N(18,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(16\\le Y\\le 20).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(16\\le Y\\le 20) & =P(\\frac{16-18}{\\sqrt{9}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{20-18}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{3}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.67\\le Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2P(Z\\ge 0.67) \\\\  & =1-2(1-P(Z\\le 0.67)) \\\\  & =2P(Z\\le 0.67))-1 \\\\  & =2Φ(0.67)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.67)=0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(16\\le Y\\le 20)=2Φ(0.67)-1=0.4972.\n$$",
