    "q-152009": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152009",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(-0.05,{0.1}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 100,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge -4.25),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{100} & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot {0.1}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(100\\cdot (-0.05),\\,100\\cdot 0.01) \\\\  & =N(-5,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge -4.25).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge -4.25) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{-4.25-(-5)}{\\sqrt{1}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.75) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.75)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge -4.25)=1-Φ(0.75)=0.2266.\n$$",
