    "q-143391": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143391",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "A bouquet consists of $20$ roses. The weight of a rose, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $70\\,\\text{g}$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\text{g}.$ If one bouquet is randomly selected, what is the probability that it will weigh more than $1\\,430\\,\\text{g}?$ You may assume that the weights of the roses in a bouquet are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th rose. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(70,{5}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected bouquet. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 70 \\\\  & =1\\,400, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =500.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,400,500).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W>1\\,430),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & =P(Z>\\frac{1\\,430-1\\,400}{\\sqrt{500}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.34) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.34) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.34)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.34)≈0.9099.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>1\\,430) & ≈1-Φ(1.34) \\\\  & ≈0.0901.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
